---

Every discounted cash flow valuation rests on two numbers you made up.
The growth forecast comes from one model, and the discount rate comes
from another. Neither is obliged to agree with the other. The course
teaches an alternative that answers the question the DCF assumes away,
which is why risky cash flows earn high expected returns, and how high. You derive both numbers from a single description of the
economy, meaning one consumption process and one set of preferences,
and the same model then prices every claim, from the aggregate stock
market to a portfolio you bring yourself.

The course is built around Kiku (2006), *Is the Value Premium a
Puzzle?*, which the site replicates in code.
Value firms out-earned growth firms by about six percentage points a
year over 1930 to 2003, while their market betas are nearly identical.
Under the CAPM, a higher return requires a higher beta, so the CAPM
cannot price the premium. By the end of the course you will have
rebuilt the general equilibrium model that resolves the puzzle, and you
will have tested it against seventy-four years of data. You will also
have used the model to price a claim of your own.

## The main result

The table below is the result the course builds toward. The model is
calibrated to consumption and dividends alone, with no return data used
anywhere in the calibration, and is then compared with the returns it
was never shown. Data columns come from the paper (Table I and
Table VII), and the code cell computes the model columns when the page
builds.

::: {#ef880138 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code code-fold="true"}
import lrrcs as lrr
import pandas as pd
from great_tables import GT

KIKU_GT = dict(table_background_color="transparent", table_font_size="0.92rem",
               heading_title_font_size="1.05rem", heading_subtitle_font_size="0.85rem",
               source_notes_font_size="0.8rem", column_labels_font_weight="bold",
               data_row_padding="4px")

model = lrr.LongRunRisksModel()
sim = model.simulate(n_samples=300, years=74, seed=0)
vg = sim.compare("value", "growth", market="market")

data_er = {"growth": 7.81, "value": 13.88, "market": 8.56}   # Kiku, Table I
names = ("growth", "value", "market")
df = pd.DataFrame({
    "claim": names,
    "data": [data_er[k] for k in names],
    "model": [sim.expected_returns[k] for k in names],
})
(
    GT(df)
    .tab_header(title="Average annual returns in the data and in the model")
    .cols_label(claim="", data="E[R] data (%)", model="E[R] model (%)")
    .fmt_number(columns=["data", "model"], decimals=2)
    .tab_source_note(f"The value premium is about 6 points in the data and "
                     f"{vg.premium:.1f} points in the model.")
    .tab_source_note(f"The CAPM beta ratio of value to growth is "
                     f"{vg.beta_ratio:.2f}, so value has the lower beta "
                     f"and the higher return.")
    .tab_options(**KIKU_GT)
)
```

